      "id": "hackernews-2026-07-03-wordgard-the-new-in-browser-rich-text-editor-from-the-creato",
      "title": "Wordgard: The new in-browser rich-text editor from the creator of ProseMirror",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://wordgard.net/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48772573\nPoints: 188\n# Comments: 74",
      "body": "Wordgard [wɜrd-gɑrd] noun A garden for cultivating words.\nOpen-source JavaScript library implementing an in-browser rich-text\nSemantic Rich Text Editor System\nWordgard provides a set of tools for building content editors. It\nis not a free-form HTML editor, but one where you control precisely\nwhat kind of content you support.\nIts main distinguishing feature is a powerful programming\ninterface that makes the library a good foundation for customized\nWordgard is open source under a permissive license\nBug reports are very welcome. Pull requests\naccepted.If you are using Wordgard\ncommercially, there is a social (but no legal) expectation\nthat you help fund its maintenance.\nStart here.Discussing the project or asking questions is best done on",
